{"node":{"id":"HP:0002072","label":"Chorea","ic":3.775,"definition":"Chorea (Greek for 'dance') refers to widespread arrhythmic involuntary movements of a forcible, jerky and restless fashion. It is a random-appearing sequence of one or more discrete involuntary movements or movement fragments. Movements appear random because of variability in timing, duration or location. Each movement may have a distinct start and end. However, movements may be strung together and thus may appear to flow randomly from one muscle group to another. Chorea can involve the trunk, neck, face, tongue, and extremities."},"diseases":[{"id":"MONDO:0014593","label":"developmental and epileptic encephalopathy, 29","in_core":true,"edge":{"id":"e_563d1f734c9f","source":"MONDO:0014593","target":"HP:0002072","type":"has_phenotype","evidence":[{"db":"HPO","origin":"OMIM","record":"OMIM:616339 · PMID:25817015","url":"https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/25817015/","level":"observed","date":"2022-03-24","confidence":1.0,"snippet":"Epileptic encephalopathy, early infantile, 29: Chorea (frequency 3/3) [PCS]"}],"contradicting":[],"confidence":1.0,"independent_sources":["OMIM"],"level":"observed"},"confidence":1.0},{"id":"MONDO:0009092","label":"polycystic lipomembranous osteodysplasia with sclerosing leukoencephaly","in_core":true,"edge":{"id":"e_3f73de2f2fbd","source":"MONDO:0009092","target":"HP:0002072","type":"has_phenotype","evidence":[{"db":"HPO","origin":"Orphanet","record":"ORPHA:2770 · ORPHA:2770","url":"https://www.orpha.net/en/disease/detail/2770","level":"curated","date":"2026-09-02","confidence":0.6,"snippet":"Nasu-Hakola disease: Chorea (frequency HP:0040282) [TAS]"},{"db":"Orphanet","origin":"Orphanet","record":"ORPHA:2770","level":"curated","date":"2026-06-23","url":"https://www.orpha.net/en/disease/detail/2770","confidence":0.6,"snippet":"Chorea — Frequent (79-30%)"}],"contradicting":[],"confidence":0.6,"independent_sources":["Orphanet"],"level":"curated"},"confidence":0.6},{"id":"MONDO:00